<BaseLayout title="Infotainment">
  <h1>Infotainment</h1>
  <p>The 2021 S60 uses Volvo's Sensus-era infotainment architecture. The center touchscreen, driver display, steering-wheel controls and optional head-up display work together, but the exact screens and functions depend on installed equipment, market and software version. This page focuses on identifying the system and diagnosing common owner-level problems without guessing at hidden software or hardware differences.</p>

  <ReferencePhoto src="https://www.volvocars.com/media/support-content/img028459637a80b16dc0a801526df36585_1_--_--_VOICEpnghigh.png" alt="Volvo Sensus-era cockpit showing driver display, steering wheel and center touchscreen" caption="Volvo Sensus interface family. Display size and optional equipment vary by vehicle." credit="Volvo Cars" source="https://www.volvocars.com/en-ca/support/car/s60/21w46/article/5b78d70bc7e244b1c0a81f6f7b69c865/5e799f698c111823c0a8cc723353898c/f6848b197ef39a36c0a80151207886f7/" license="Volvo Cars support illustration" />

  <h2>What makes up the system?</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Role</th><th>Fitment note</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><th>Center display</th><td>Touch interface for climate, media, phone, navigation and vehicle settings.</td><td>Core Sensus interface; screen functions depend on software and equipment.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Driver display</th><td>Shows speed, warning messages, trip information and selected media/phone information.</td><td>Volvo describes both 8-inch and 12-inch instrument-panel versions across the platform; confirm the fitted version.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Head-up display</th><td>Projects selected driving information onto the windshield.</td><td>Optional equipment; not fitted to every S60.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Steering-wheel keypads</th><td>Operate driver-display information, audio and supported voice/phone functions.</td><td>Button behavior can depend on the active screen and installed features.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Audio hardware</th><td>Radio, media playback, phone audio and warning chimes.</td><td>Sound-system brand and speaker configuration vary by build.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <p>Volvo describes Sensus as the interface for entertainment, internet connectivity and navigation. The center display also controls functions outside infotainment, including climate settings and some vehicle settings. See <a href="https://www.volvocars.com/en-ca/support/car/s60/21w46/article/5b78d70bc7e244b1c0a81f6f7b69c865/5e799f698c111823c0a8cc723353898c/f6848b197ef39a36c0a80151207886f7/">Volvo's Sensus overview</a>.</p>

  <h2>Center display views</h2>
  <p>The interface organizes functions into Home, Top, Climate, App and Function views. On a left-hand-drive vehicle, the normal swipe directions are described from the driver's seated position; right-hand-drive layouts are mirrored. Some functions and tiles appear only when the vehicle is equipped for them.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Home view:</strong> overview tiles for commonly used functions such as navigation, media and phone. Tap a tile to expand it.</li>
    <li><strong>Top view:</strong> settings, driver profiles, stored messages and access to the owner's information, depending on software version.</li>
    <li><strong>Climate view:</strong> temperature, fan, defrost and available seat/steering-wheel climate controls.</li>
    <li><strong>App view:</strong> installed vehicle apps and supported downloaded apps.</li>
    <li><strong>Function view:</strong> vehicle-function controls for features actually installed on the car.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not assume a generic screenshot proves that a particular button or feature is installed. Volvo's <a href="https://www.volvocars.com/en-ca/support/car/s60/20w46/article/8d5ce21f8c14b3a6c0a8cc7221c68fd0/16c8c292c7e5c384c0a81f6f7f757087/d755db0b7ec0c6efc0a8015144008437/">2021 S60 center-display guide</a> is the better reference for view navigation.</p>

  <h2>Driver profiles and settings</h2>
  <p>Driver profiles can retain selected personal settings. If mirrors, seat position, media, language or other preferences seem to change unexpectedly, first check which profile is active and whether the vehicle is associating the expected key with that profile. Not every setting is profile-specific, and available memory functions depend on the installed seat and vehicle equipment.</p>
  <p>Settings categories and menu wording can change with software. Record the software version and photograph the relevant menu before troubleshooting a setting that repeatedly resets.</p>

  <h2>Bluetooth, USB and phone projection</h2>
  <p>Bluetooth pairing, USB media and phone projection are separate connection paths. Diagnose them separately rather than treating every phone problem as a Bluetooth fault.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Confirm the phone's Bluetooth and permissions are enabled, then check whether the vehicle lists it as a paired device.</li>
    <li>If pairing fails, remove the stale pairing from both the phone and vehicle, restart the phone, and pair again while parked.</li>
    <li>For a wired data connection, use a known-good data-capable USB cable. A charge-only cable can power a phone without carrying data.</li>
    <li>Use the USB port specified by the owner's manual for the desired function. USB port roles can differ; rear charging ports should not be assumed to support media or phone projection.</li>
    <li>Record the phone model, operating-system version, cable, USB port and exact on-screen message when reporting a connection problem.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Apple CarPlay and Android Auto support must be confirmed for the specific market, model-year software and installed USB hardware. Do not infer compatibility from the presence of a USB socket alone. Check the 2021 U.S. owner's information and the phone-projection instructions for the vehicle before purchasing adapters or replacing hardware.</p>

  <h2>Audio and media fault isolation</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>First distinction to make</th><th>Useful evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><th>No sound from any source</th><td>Does the radio, turn signal, navigation and parking-assistance audio all fail, or only one media source?</td><td>Whether warning chimes also disappear; whether the display is frozen or rebooting.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>One source is silent</th><td>Compare radio, Bluetooth audio, USB media and phone calls.</td><td>Selected source, source volume, mute state and whether another phone behaves the same way.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Volume knob appears to respond but volume does not change</th><td>Check whether the fault is limited to the current media source.</td><td>Try another source, then return to the original source.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Voice control stops recognizing contacts</th><td>Check whether the issue began after pairing or syncing a phone.</td><td>Contact names containing emoji or unusual special characters can interfere with phonetic matching.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Screen freezes or repeatedly restarts</th><td>Determine whether audio and warning sounds are also affected.</td><td>Record when it started, recent battery events, software version and whether it recurs after the vehicle has fully gone to sleep.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  <h2>Volvo's documented Sensus recovery guidance</h2>
  <p>Volvo's U.S. Sensus troubleshooting article applies to S60 and S60 Recharge model years 2019–2022. For the documented case where the center display is frozen/rebooting or there is no sound from the radio, turn signals, navigation or park assist, Volvo instructs the owner to:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Stop the vehicle.</li>
    <li>Lock the vehicle.</li>
    <li>Allow the vehicle to enter sleep mode. Volvo says this occurs after approximately 30 minutes.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Volvo notes that the display should then work properly. For the specific volume-knob symptom, Volvo suggests switching to another media source and then switching back. For voice-control problems that begin after pairing a phone, Volvo identifies emoji or special characters in contact names as a possible cause. These are symptom-specific suggestions, not a universal reset procedure.</p>
  <p><strong>Do not start with a factory reset.</strong> Volvo says a factory reset clears settings and requires reconnecting phones; consult the vehicle's owner's information for the correct procedure if simpler checks do not resolve the fault. Volvo also recommends discussing a possible software update with a workshop when issues persist. <a href="https://www.volvocars.com/us/support/topic/cd93596a467f7026c0a80189614d7ef2/60efc3ce468059ffc0a801892b78a7b0/da138f3d4680c68bc0a801897f875456/4b1887d95c2cc86cc0a801517cf0c655/">Read Volvo's Sensus troubleshooting article</a>.</p>

  <h2>Internet and connected services</h2>
  <p>Internet access and connected services depend on market, subscription, cellular coverage, account status and the services still supported for the vehicle. A phone hotspot, Bluetooth tethering and the vehicle's integrated modem are not interchangeable assumptions. If an app or online service stops working, record the exact error and check service availability and account status before changing vehicle settings.</p>

  <h2>What to record before seeking help</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Model year, market and fitted audio-system name (if known).</li>
    <li>Vehicle software version and whether the display freezes, reboots or remains responsive.</li>
    <li>Exact symptoms: one source silent, all audio silent, warning chimes missing, phone pairing failure or app/network failure.</li>
    <li>Phone make/model, operating-system version, connection type, USB port and cable.</li>
    <li>Whether the issue started after a battery replacement, software update, phone update or other repair.</li>
  </ul>

